entries.methodology:
An active prospect flags as Breakout when ALL of the following hold over the window max(last 6 months, last 4 source updates):

(a) consensus FV upgraded by ≥ +5 grade points OR mean tool-grade composite improved by ≥ +3;

(b) a strong performance gate fires - hitters: RPS+ ≥ 110 or wRC+ ≥ 130; pitchers: Peripheral Stuff+ ≥ 105 or FIP- ≤ 90 (v29 - Statcast+ ≥ 115 also passes as an alternative gate);

(c) v15 age cap - position players and SPs through age 24; relievers (RP / MIRP / SIRP / CL) through age 25 (relievers peak earlier in their development arc, so an aging-relief hot stretch shouldn't read as a prospect breakout).

Tuned to surface ~10-40 prospects league-wide. The badge gets an aquamarine pulsing ★ mark (bottom-left corner) with a tooltip explaining which signal fired and which gate passed.

An externally built model lineage scores each pitcher's pitch types from TrackMan kinematics against run value, then rescales to a class-relative index shown per pitch type on the pitcher page. Governance record and coverage live in the registry (collegesavant_stuff row, eval artifact data/collegesavant_stuff_eval.json): seasons 2024-2026, thousands of pitchers per full season, agreement r 0.96 with the sibling vintage vendored in the repo.

SCALE COLLISION WARNING. Six different things are called some form of Stuff+ in this tree and NONE of them share a scale, so a number from one is meaningless against a number from another:
(1) this CollegeSavant display scale (college TrackMan xRV ratio, 100 = college average, 0 = worst observed, measured SD about 23 to 24);
(2) Will's 2026 formula-anchored vintage (same formula family, a DIFFERENT model fit, vendored as a separate payload);
(3) the platform's aStuff+ (in-house AAA model, 100 + 10z vs the qualified AAA pool);
(4) tjStuff+ (Thomas Nestico's external per-pitch MLB model, scored as 100 minus 10z and clamped 20 to 180, so its sign convention is inverted relative to the others);
(5) pStuff+ as computed in Python for the served statline (peripheral K% / BB% / HR-9 vs level mean, 100 + 10z);
(6) pStuff+ as computed in the PROSPX leaderboard JS (same family, reconciled onto the Python formula 2026-07-29).
A 130 here is barely plus; a 130 aStuff+ is elite. They are never mixed on one surface.

A gated test of this signal as a college-to-pro K% translation covariate FAILED (zero forward signal on 257 matched pairs) so it remains display context only.
